On Oct 23, 7:14 am, Patric Mueller <bh...@bigfoot.com> wrote:

> Many people have criticized Sokoban's design[1] and said it doesn't
> really fit into the game. I'm not one of the Sokoban haters but I too
> think it's not perfect.

Nothing about NetHack is entirely perfect.  However, in general,
I am not in favor of excising Sokoban from the game.  It is indeed
somewhat _different_ from the other branches, but I don't see that
as a bad thing.  The loot, while substantial, is not so compelling
that players _can't_ skip it if they really dislike it.

I'm not even in favor of removing or outright replacing Gehennom,
and it makes Sokoban look like a paragon of fantastic game design.

I prefer less radical forms of improvement -- incremental changes
that leave NetHack still recognizable as NetHack but make it better.

> Disallowing bones is a logical workaround for a level
> where there's only one true way to solve it and every
> other creative solution will be punished. Which is
> completely contrary to the rest of the game.

Disallowing bones is the quick and easy workaround.
I believe allowing bones but ensuring that the boulders
and pits are in a normalized state is a better workaround.
This preserves the special nature of Sokoban, leaves it
completable, but allows for characters who die there to
be encountered later by someone else, just as in other
dungeon branches.

> Remove the luck penalties from Sokoban and replace
> them with a solved-Sokoban-correctly tracking and
> enable bones for the Sokoban levels.

I think removing the luck penalties from Sokoban
is a more radical change.

> [1] For example, Derek Ray is on record for wanting to
>     throw Sokoban out of SporkHack completely because
>     of various reasons but not doing it because he had
>     no suitable replacement.

SporkHack is an interesting variant, but it is not a
particularly conservative one in terms of gameplay
changes.  It's fine that there are variants like that,
but my proposal was intended to be more conservative
and thus potentially acceptable to a wider audience,
possibly even for eventual inclusion in vanilla.


